==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|enquire}} {{qualifier|chiefly British}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|inquīrō||to seek for}}. Displaced {{cog|enm|enqueren}} (from {{cog|fro|enquerre}}, of the same source) and native {{cog|ang|speir||ask, inquire}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ɪnˈkwaɪɹ/|lang=en}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ɪnˈkwaɪə/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-inquire.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪə(ɹ)|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|in|quire|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|inquires|inquiring|inquired}} {{tcx|ambitransitive|lang=en}}

# {{lb|en|US}} To [[ask]] (about something).
# To make an [[inquiry]] or an [[investigation]].
#* {{RQ:EHough PrqsPrc|I|0124}}
#*: "A fine man, that Dunwody, yonder," commented the young captain, as they parted, and as he turned to his prisoner. "We'll see him on in Washington some day.{{...}}A strong man&mdash;a strong one; and a heedless." ¶ "Of what party is he?" she '''inquired''', as though casually.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[call]]; to [[name]].
#* Spenser
#*: Canute had his portion from the rest, The which he call'd Canutium, for his hire, Now Cantium, which Kent we commonly inquire.

====Usage notes====
* In British English, the spelling '''[[enquire]]''' is more common, with '''inquire''' often reserved for official [[inquest]]s. In Canada and the US, both spellings are acceptable, though '''inquire''' is favored.

